<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuwait</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as State of Kuwait</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuwait, officially the State of Kuwait, is a country in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. In the early 18th century its territory was under the Bani Khalid clan; it became known as the Sheikdom of Kuwait in 1752 and a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1899. Before oil reserves were discovered in 1938 it was a regional trade port. The protectorate agreements with the United Kingdom ended in June 1961, when Kuwait became an independent state. In 1990 Ba'athist Iraq under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> invaded it, installed a puppet regime and annexed it. The occupation ended on 26 February 1991, when a U.S.-led coalition expelled Iraqi forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-war">Gulf War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuwait.jpg" alt="Kuwait" width="330" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kuwait.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kuwait lies at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> in the north-eastern corner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, with a coastline of about 500 kilometres. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to the north and Saudi Arabia to the south and is one of the smallest countries in the world by land area. The land is generally low-lying, and the highest point, Mutla Ridge, is 306 metres above sea level. Much of present-day Kuwait was formed from debris laid down by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> after the post-glacial flooding of the Gulf basin, and historically most of it was part of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Kuwait has no permanent rivers, though Bubiyan Island lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab">Shatt al-Arab</a> delta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>One of the earliest signs of human presence dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> era, about 8000 BCE. In the early to mid-1700s Kuwait City was a small fishing village and a sheikhdom under the Bani Khalid. Some time in the mid-1700s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bani-utbah">Bani Utbah</a> settled there, and after the fall of the Bani Khalid Emirate they took control through successive marriage alliances. Oil was first exported in 1946, and from 1950 a major public-works programme was begun. The period from 1946 to 1980 is known as the golden era of Kuwait. The suspension of parliament in 1976 drew protests from home-grown democrats.</p><p>In the 1980s Kuwait suffered pro-Iranian attacks during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a> because of its financial support to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. In 1990 Ba'athist Iraq under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> invaded it, installed a puppet regime and annexed it after disputes over oil production. The occupation ended on 26 February 1991 when an international coalition led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> expelled the Iraqi forces. In the early 1990s Kuwait deported nearly 400,000 Palestinians, in response to the alignment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">PLO</a> with Saddam Hussein, as well as thousands of Iraqis and Yemenis. Many stateless residents known as bedoon were also expelled, and the Bedoon have since been the subject of criticism from human rights organisations and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kuwait is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>, and its constitution makes the emir an heir of Mubarak Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>. Kuwait City is the capital and largest city, and the country is divided into six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a>. The official language is Modern Standard Arabic, while Kuwaiti Arabic is used in daily life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, specifically the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>, is the state religion, and expatriates make up about 60 percent of the population. Kuwait is a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-cooperation-council">Gulf Cooperation Council</a> and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">OPEC</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kuwait” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Failaka Island off <b>Kuwait</b>, with its Dilmun-period temple and seal workshop, was part of the same political and commercial sphere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Karbala in 1802 and took Mecca in 1803 before the Ottoman viceroy of Egypt, Mohammed Ali Pasha, destroyed it in 1818; the smaller Emirate of Nejd of 1824 lost the interior to the Al Rashid of Jabal Shammar, and by 1891 the Al Saud were exiles in <b>Kuwait</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Emirati units joined the coalition that expelled Iraq from <b>Kuwait</b> in 1991, served in Somalia, Kosovo and Afghanistan, and flew in the 2011 air campaign over Libya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A clay disc from the site of H3 in <b>Kuwait</b> shows a two-masted boat, the oldest evidence for the use of masts and sails.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ubaid-culture">Ubaid culture · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Some holdouts remained: Liechtenstein enfranchised women in 1984, the canton of Appenzell Innerrhoden was compelled to admit them to its assembly by the Federal Supreme Court in 1990, and women first voted in <b>Kuwait</b>’s parliamentary elections in 2006 and in Saudi Arabia’s municipal elections in 2015.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Several other monarchies, among them Jordan, <b>Kuwait</b> and Morocco, have adopted constitutions while their monarchs keep wide powers, and in Liechtenstein a referendum in 2003 enlarged the prince’s powers to dismiss the government and to veto legislation.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
